m1	505,318,1475,628		Two columns of chord diagrams: g + b₁ - d | f resolving to g ... c + e₁ and c + e₁ - g + b₁ to c ... f + a₁; e₁ - g + b₁ - d to e₁ ... a₁ - c and b₁ - d | f + a₁ to b₁ ... e₁ - g, lines joining each note to its successor.
m2	740,786,1145,940		Scheme in Roman numerals: I - III - V - VII over II - IV - VI, lines shewing a Seventh resolving so that the descending VII becomes the Fifth of the new chord.
m3	511,1054,1430,1222		The chords b₁ - d | f + a resolving to c + e₁ - g, and b₁ - d | f - a¹♭ resolving to c - e¹♭ + g, lines joining the notes, the only natural cases of this scheme.
m4	570,1419,1415,1585		The so-called false cadences: g + b₁ - d | f resolving to a₁ - c + e₁, and g + b₁ - d | f resolving to a¹♭ + c - e¹♭, lines joining the notes.
m5	445,1952,1537,2106		Two schemes in Roman numerals with the Seventh unchanged: I - III - V - VII over II - IV - VII, marked †, and I - III - V - VII over II - V - VII, marked ‡.
m6	708,2323,1270,2480		Scheme in Roman numerals: I - III - V - VII joined by vertical lines to VII - III - V - VII, marked §, where the Seventh would become the Fifth of the new chord.
m7	276,2645,825,2766		Footnote examples of the second method: g + b₁ - d | f resolving to g + b₁ ... e₁, and e₁ - g + b₁ - d resolving to e₁ - g ... c, lines joining the notes.
m8	200,2837,892,2956		Footnote examples: g + b₁ - d | f to a₁ - c ... f, c + e₁ - g + b₁ to d | f ... b₁, and e₁ - g + b₁ - d to f + a₁ ... d, lines joining the notes.
m9	1175,2703,1650,2823		Footnote examples: g + b₁ - d | f resolving to a₁ d f, and c + e₁ - g + b₁ resolving to d ... g + b₁, lines joining the notes.
m10	1155,2902,1655,3022		Footnote examples: g + b₁ - d | f joined by vertical lines to f ... b₁ - d | f, and c + e₁ - g + b₁ to b₁ ... e₁ - g + b₁.
